rdpserver release notes

Unreleased (0.0.9, Phase J)
	NLA / CredSSP / NTLMv2 framework, NOT advertised yet.

	Honest scoping: NLA cryptographically requires server-side
	access to the user's NT hash (or a credential proxy that
	knows it).  A standalone Linux/BSD daemon with PAM/bsd_auth
	cannot decrypt the sealed TSCredentials without that hash;
	the cleartext password never reaches us in the clear.
	Production deployments need a hash backend -- winbind, sssd,
	or a custom PAM module.  Until such a backend is wired in,
	enabling NLA would only let us reach the authInfo decryption
	step and fail.  The daemon therefore does NOT advertise
	PROTOCOL_HYBRID in its X.224 negotiate response; xfreerdp
	/sec:nla sees a clean negotiation failure and retries with
	/sec:tls.

	The wire and crypto pieces are in the tree for future use:

	src/sec/nla_crypto.{c,h}: MD4 (NT hash), HMAC-MD5 (NTLMv2
	hash + NTProofStr + sealing keys), stateful RC4 (TSCredentials
	sealing/unsealing).  Thin EVP_*/HMAC wrappers around OpenSSL
	or LibreSSL.  OpenSSL 3.0 deprecation warning is silenced
	with a pragma; LibreSSL doesn't ship EVP_MAC yet so we stay
	on the classic HMAC API.

	src/sec/ntlm.{c,h}: NEGOTIATE/CHALLENGE/AUTHENTICATE wire
	format from MS-NLMP.  ntlm_build_challenge generates a fresh
	8-byte server challenge, picks compatible negotiate flags
	(UNICODE + NTLM + EXTENDED_SESSIONSECURITY + 128 +
	KEY_EXCH + TARGET_INFO), serialises a CHALLENGE_MESSAGE
	with NB computer/domain names + RPC timestamp.
	ntlm_verify_ntlmv2 computes NTHash -> NTLMv2Hash ->
	NTProofStr and constant-time-compares against the client's
	first 16 bytes of the NT response; on a match it derives the
	SessionBaseKey.  ntlm_derive_exported_key RC4-unwraps the
	EncryptedRandomSessionKey when KEY_EXCH is set.

	src/sec/cssp.{c,h}: TSRequest DER builder + parser using the
	existing BER helpers, with context-tagged components 0..3
	(version, negoTokens, authInfo, pubKeyAuth).  A separate
	parser handles TSCredentials -> TSPasswordCreds.

	src/sec/nla.{c,h}: the server-side state machine that
	stitches the above together.  Today it parses NEGOTIATE,
	sends CHALLENGE, parses AUTHENTICATE, then fails at the
	authInfo decryption step with a logged warning explaining
	what backend is missing.

	Fuzz coverage: the existing in-tree fuzzer gained cssp and
	ntlm drivers (regress/fuzz/fuzz_parsers).  100 000 random-
	byte iterations per parser under ASan + UBSan: zero crashes
	across all 9 parsers (tpkt, x224, ber, per, mcs, cliprdr,
	fp_input, cssp, ntlm).

	New docs/SECURITY.md documents the trust model, the
	privilege-separation layout, why NLA is currently a
	framework-only feature, and the operator hook (a
	`rdp_nla_get_nt_hash(user, domain, out_hash)` function) that
	a hash backend would implement.

Unreleased (0.0.8, Phase I)
	Hardening sweep.

	`./configure --enable-sanitizers` adds -fsanitize=address,
	undefined to CFLAGS+LDFLAGS, drops _FORTIFY_SOURCE which
	conflicts, and forces -O0 -g3 -fno-omit-frame-pointer.  The
	full regress suite passes under ASan+UBSan on Linux/aarch64;
	no leaks, no UB diagnostics.

	regress/fuzz/fuzz_parsers: in-tree mini-fuzzer driving each
	parser with random bytes of varying lengths (xorshift32 PRNG,
	user-seedable for reproducibility).  Covers TPKT, X.224
	(parse_cr + parse_dt), BER (length + universal/app tag +
	integer/boolean/enumerated/octet-string), PER (length +
	choice + OID + u16/u32 + h221_key + user_data_count), MCS
	(parse_connect_initial + all DomainPDU parsers), CLIPRDR
	(parse_hdr + parse_format_list both modes + parse_format_
	data_request), and fast-path input.  2.1 million iterations
	across three seeds under ASan+UBSan: zero crashes, zero
	out-of-bounds, zero UB diagnostics.  `make fuzz` runs a
	5000-iteration smoke pass over every parser.

	pledge(2) on OpenBSD: each binary now drops to the minimum
	promise set after its setup phase:
	  - rdpd worker (after fork, before run_proxy):
	      stdio inet unix
	  - rdp-sessionmgr (after bind):
	      stdio rpath wpath cpath unix proc exec id getpw
	      dpath fattr
	  - rdp-session (after Xvfb + xterm spawn):
	      stdio rpath wpath cpath unix proc
	The full live chain (xfreerdp from Linux -> OpenBSD daemon ->
	bsd_auth -> rdp-session -> Xvfb -> xterm -> back) still works
	with these pledges active; the listener correctly stays
	unpledged so it can accept + fork + setuid.  On non-OpenBSD
	the pledge calls compile to a no-op via the compat.h shim.

	Static analysis: cppcheck pass surfaced no correctness bugs.
	A dead inner struct in src/session/clip_x11.c was removed.

	Out of scope for this phase: clang/libFuzzer (no clang on
	the dev box), 24 CPU-hours of fuzzing per parser, multi-OS
	CI matrix (no infra), and the Linux seccomp-bpf / FreeBSD
	capsicum allow-lists -- the configure script does detect
	those primitives so wiring them is straightforward when
	the rest is ready.

Unreleased (0.0.7, Phase H)
	Clean disconnect.  The outbound MCS Disconnect Provider
	Ultimatum was wrapped in a Send Data Indication, which the
	spec does not permit -- the PDU is its own MCS choice (8),
	not part of a Send Data exchange.  Added
	src/daemon/conn.c::send_mcs_raw that wraps a bare MCS PDU
	in X.224 DT + TPKT only, and routed the disconnect through
	it.  On the wire the server now emits exactly 9 bytes
	(TPKT 4 + X.224 DT 3 + MCS 2) on session end, which
	Microsoft RDP clients and xfreerdp accept silently.

	Shutdown Request PDU: when the client sends PDUTYPE2_SHUTDOWN_
	REQUEST (mstsc's Ctrl+Alt+End maps to this), the worker now
	replies with PDUTYPE2_SHUTDOWN_DENIED so the client follows
	up with a graceful MCS Disconnect rather than a hard close.
	Detection lives in maybe_dispatch_clip alongside the CLIPRDR
	dispatcher (the I/O channel filter catches it).

	TCP keepalive on accepted sockets via SO_KEEPALIVE plus
	per-OS TCP_KEEPIDLE/INTVL/CNT (60s/10s/6) where the headers
	are present.  Half-open TCP connections are detected within
	about two minutes of silence, so the worker exits even if
	the client process is killed without a TCP FIN.

	DRDYNVC: minimal stub kept from earlier phases.  The channel
	is joined at MCS connect; the daemon never initiates any
	Capabilities Request, so xfreerdp doesn't try to open
	dynamic channels and the channel sits silent for the
	connection lifetime.  Full DRDYNVC support is a v2 item.

	Verified live on OpenBSD: with rdp-session, Xvfb :100, xterm,
	and a user shell all running, killing the xfreerdp process
	from the local side caused the worker to detect TLS EOF,
	emit the MCS Disconnect, close TLS, close the backend fd;
	rdp-session saw EOF on its backend fd, terminated Xvfb and
	xterm, and exited; rdp-sessionmgr's SIGCHLD reaper collected
	them.  After teardown, `ps -fU rdptest` showed no remaining
	processes; only rdpd and rdp-sessionmgr listeners stayed up.

Unreleased (0.0.6, Phase G)
	CLIPRDR: MS-RDPECLIP static virtual channel, text formats only
	(CF_UNICODETEXT) for v1.  Bidirectional: copy text in the
	per-user xterm and it lands in the local clipboard; copy on
	the client side and paste in the xterm.

	src/channels/cliprdr.{c,h}: PDU builders/parsers for
	CB_MONITOR_READY, CB_CLIP_CAPS, CB_FORMAT_LIST (long format
	names path), CB_FORMAT_LIST_RESPONSE, CB_FORMAT_DATA_REQUEST,
	CB_FORMAT_DATA_RESPONSE.  Inbound format list parser
	recognises both the long and short forms.  UTF-16LE <-> UTF-8
	conversion uses src/common/utf16.

	conn.c: identifies the CLIPRDR channel at MCS connect time by
	walking the CS_NET channel list, dispatches incoming SDRs on
	that channel to the cliprdr handler, sends Monitor Ready and
	Caps to the client after activation, wraps outgoing CLIPRDR
	PDUs in CHANNEL_PDU_HEADER + Send Data Indication + X.224 DT
	+ TPKT.  No reassembly path yet (a single FIRST|LAST chunk
	carries each PDU); fragmentation is a follow-up.

	Backend RPC gains three message types: CLIP_OFFER (announce
	available formats), CLIP_REQUEST (ask for a format), CLIP_DATA
	(deliver the bytes).  Worker translates these to CLIPRDR PDUs
	and vice-versa.

	src/session/clip_x11.{c,h}: X11 selection bridge in
	rdp-session.  XFixesSelectSelectionInput on CLIPBOARD detects
	owner changes coming from xterm or other X clients; we then
	XConvertSelection for UTF8_STRING into a scratch property,
	read the bytes on SelectionNotify, and send CLIP_OFFER +
	(later) CLIP_DATA to the worker.  On CLIP_OFFER from the
	worker we claim CLIPBOARD via XSetSelectionOwner; on
	SelectionRequest we answer the TARGETS query immediately and
	defer text targets until CLIP_DATA arrives, replying with
	XChangeProperty + a synthetic SelectionNotify.

	The +clipboard flag must be passed to xfreerdp for the local
	side to mirror the RDP clipboard into the local CLIPBOARD
	selection.

	Verified live on OpenBSD: xclip on display :100 in the
	rdp-session ksh shell triggers a XFixesSelectionNotify ->
	CLIP_OFFER -> CB_FORMAT_LIST -> CB_FORMAT_DATA_REQUEST ->
	CLIP_REQUEST -> CLIP_DATA -> CB_FORMAT_DATA_RESPONSE round
	trip; the text appears in the local Xvfb's CLIPBOARD where
	xfreerdp deposited it.  The reverse direction works the same
	way.

Unreleased (0.0.5, Phase F)
	rdp-session: a new binary, exec'd by rdp-sessionmgr after
	SPAWN authenticates and drops privileges to the target user.
	Spawns Xvfb on the first free DISPLAY in :100-:200, opens
	it as an X client, sets up MIT-SHM XShm capture and XTest
	input injection, then launches xterm.  Captures the root
	window every 200 ms and pushes a FRAME backend message.
	Reads INPUT_KEY/INPUT_MOUSE messages from the worker and
	injects them via XTestFakeKeyEvent / XTestFakeButtonEvent.

	Backend RPC: src/backend/proto.{h,c}.  AF_UNIX SOCK_STREAM
	socket pair set up by sessmgr at SPAWN.  Length-prefixed
	frames: HELLO_S2W (mode), FRAME (BGR 24bpp pixels), INPUT_KEY,
	INPUT_MOUSE, BYE.

	rdp-sessionmgr SPAWN op (was stubbed in Phase E): handler
	verifies prior AUTH on the same connection, looks up the
	authenticated user via getpwnam, fork()s, in the child does
	initgroups + setresgid + setresuid, sets HOME/USER/SHELL/PATH
	(now including /usr/X11R6/bin), exec()s rdp-session with the
	socketpair on fd 3.  Returns the other end of the pair to
	the worker via SCM_RIGHTS in the SPAWN reply.

	Sessmgr connection model is now multi-op: a single connection
	does AUTH then SPAWN, with the authenticated username
	remembered between the two.  Closing the connection forgets
	the auth.  Bug: the global SIGCHLD reaper raced with PAM
	and bsd_auth's own waitpid() for their helper processes;
	handle_auth now sets SIGCHLD to SIG_DFL around the
	`rdp_auth_user` call and restores it afterwards.

	rdpd worker: post-login proxy loop in conn.c.  After greeter
	OK, calls rdp_sessmgr_spawn, receives backend fd, polls
	{tls_fd, be_fd}.  TLS fast-path input -> INPUT_KEY/MOUSE
	backend messages.  Backend FRAME messages -> tiled fast-path
	bitmap updates over the same TLS connection.

	Configure: probes for libX11, libXdamage, libXext, libXfixes,
	libXtst.  Picks /usr/X11R6/include and -L/usr/X11R6/lib on
	the BSDs.  Finds Xvfb in PATH or /usr/X11R6/bin and embeds
	the path as RDP_XVFB_PATH at compile time.

	Verified live on OpenBSD: rdp-sessionmgr (root via doas) +
	rdpd (non-root) + xfreerdp from the Linux box.  A real shell
	for user rdptest renders inside the xfreerdp window;
	captured screenshot shows the xterm prompt "amd64$" with
	the X root stipple, proving the full pipeline from xfreerdp
	through TLS, the greeter, sessmgr AUTH+SPAWN, rdp-session,
	Xvfb, and back.

Unreleased (0.0.4, Phase E)
	rdp-sessionmgr: a separate privileged daemon that owns
	authentication.  AF_UNIX SOCK_SEQPACKET socket; each
	request/response is one atomic datagram.  One op for v1:
	AUTH user pass -> ok | fail.  SPAWN remains stubbed for
	Phase F (X session helper).

	Auth backends are picked by configure: PAM on Linux,
	FreeBSD and NetBSD; bsd_auth on OpenBSD.  Linux PAM uses
	the "login" service by default, overridable via -S; the
	conversation callback feeds the password to
	PAM_PROMPT_ECHO_OFF.  OpenBSD uses `auth_userokay(3)` and
	links against `-lutil`.

	Worker side: rdpd now takes -S sock_path.  conn.c plumbs
	the path into rdp_greeter_run via the auth_fn hook the
	greeter already had; when -S is omitted the previous
	"accept any non-empty pair" stub stays in place for
	development.  The password buffer makes one trip from
	greeter -> worker -> sessmgr socket and is then
	explicit_bzero'd on both ends.

	Verified live: the OpenBSD daemon, with rdp-sessionmgr
	running under doas, rejects wrong passwords for the fuzz
	user and accepts the correct password for a throwaway
	rdptest user (bcrypt-hashed via encrypt(1), wiped after
	the test).  xfreerdp on the Linux box drives the full
	connection; greeter shows "Authentication failed" in red
	on rejection.

Unreleased (0.0.3, Phase D)
	Greeter: a login dialog drawn entirely via fast-path bitmap
	updates (no drawing orders required, since the Demand Active
	negotiates none).  Centred panel with "Username" and "Password"
	fields, a Login button, and a status line.  Focus is cycled by
	Tab; Enter advances Username -> Password and submits from
	Password or the button.  Backspace deletes; Esc cancels.
	Password is masked as bullets and `explicit_bzero`'d on every
	transition out of SUBMITTING.

	The font is an 8x16 PSF (public-domain Linux console face,
	extracted by tools/mkfont.py).  US-layout scancode->ASCII map
	in keymap.c; per-LCID layouts are a follow-up.  Authentication
	is pluggable via a function pointer in rdp_greeter_run; the
	default stub accepts any non-empty user/password pair.  Real
	PAM/bsd_auth wiring lives in Phase E.

	On submit the daemon's conn.c falls through from the greeter
	to the previous Phase B/C post-login behaviour (solid frame
	plus logged input events).

	Verified live on the OpenBSD VM via xfreerdp: dialog renders,
	Username and Password fields accept typed input, Tab moves
	focus, password input shows as bullets.  Screenshots captured
	in regress/integ for reference.

Unreleased (0.0.2, Phase A)
	Full RDP handshake implemented from scratch in C: TPKT (RFC 1006),
	X.224 Class 0 with RDP_NEG_REQ/RSP, TLS server-side via OpenSSL
	with on-the-fly self-signed cert generation, MCS Connect Initial
	parse and Connect Response build (BER application tag 102 plus a
	GCC Conference Create Response with SC_CORE / SC_SECURITY /
	SC_NET blocks), Erect Domain Request, Attach User Confirm,
	Channel Join Request/Confirm loop, RDP Security header + Client
	Info PDU parse, "Valid Client" license alert, Demand Active with
	the full set of mandatory caps (drawing orders disabled, glyph
	cache disabled, bitmap cache empty, multifragment 0xFFFF, large
	pointer 96x96), Confirm Active parse, finalization handshake
	(Synchronize, Control Cooperate, Granted Control, Font Map).
	The daemon then sends a fast-path Synchronize, System Pointer
	Default, and a tiled solid bitmap update.  Fast-path input
	(scancode, mouse, MouseX, sync, Unicode) is parsed and logged.
	Clean disconnect on inbound MCS Disconnect Provider Ultimatum;
	outbound disconnect on session end.

	rdpd forks a worker per connection; the worker runs the state
	machine, the parent reaps SIGCHLD.  Per-connection TLS uses a
	shared SSL_CTX inherited via fork.  Self-signed certificate
	generated to tmp/server.{crt,key} on first run.

	Stubs in place for Phase D (greeter), Phase E (sessionmgr +
	PAM), Phase F (rdp-session + Xvfb backend), Phase G (CLIPRDR),
	and Phase J (NLA/CredSSP).  Backend vtable defined in
	src/include/rdp_backend.h.

	Verified via regress/integ/connect_test.py: full path through
	TLS handshake, MCS Connect, Attach User, Channel Join.

Unreleased (0.0.1, Phase 0)
	Project skeleton.  Hand-rolled configure script probes for libtls
	or OpenSSL, getrandom or arc4random, PAM or bsd_auth, and epoll
	or kqueue.  Baseline rdpd daemon accepts connections on a listen
	port (default 3389) and closes them immediately, logging each
	accept.  Common library: bounded buffers, read/write helpers,
	syslog wrapper, allocation wrappers, random byte source, string
	and UTF-16 helpers.  Man page stubs for rdpd(8), rdpd.conf(5),
	rdp-sessionmgr(8), and rdp-session(8).  Regress harness with
	unit tests for the common library.  Builds on Linux (glibc);
	targets OpenBSD, FreeBSD, NetBSD with stub adaptation paths.
